ed  and so it's um it's it's the difference  between  giving an undertaking that's quite  normal in a trade agreement that  um you're not going to sort of diminish  standards on  technical standards on products or  indeed environmental standards  but without fettering your ability as an  independent country  to make your own laws and that's  something that we won't do  what's the situation on phishing because  it has sounded like you're an awfully  long way  apart what exactly is the eu demanding  now  and what are we asking for  well look the big problem with the  common fisheries  policy is it's been profoundly unfair to  uk fishermen  we currently only under eu law are  allowed to  catch half of the fish in our waters the  other half has always been given away  to other eu countries and what we've  been clear about is as we  leave the european union we want to  become like norway and control  access to our waters who can come in on  what terms  and also to use that access to  rebalance the sharing arrangement  towards something that's more modern and  more scientific  and crucially fairer to our own  fishermen and that's all we've been  asking for  well i suppose not surprisingly um you  know the european union would like to  have  the same privileges of access that they  have now  but that's simply not going to be  possible when we become an independent  coastal state so  you know what can be done here is um  it's possible and there's precedent in  normal fisheries agreements for there to  be a multi-annual agreement of perhaps  up to three years  where there's an understanding around  access and sharing that happens for  instance already  with mackerel in the north sea but um  what you can't do is give some sort of  treaty guarantee  of access that again you know violates  the the basic principle that  we should be able to control who have  access to our waters and under what  terms  so just to get into the specifics uh  because it's quite you know just trying 